-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### Eviction Policy
+
+The cache uses 2Q eviction so that a long sequential screening streams through without displacing the smaller comparison set. Cache entries occupy one of two queues:
+
+- A **probationary queue**, FIFO-ordered, holds sheets that have been admitted but not yet requested again.
+- A **protected queue**, LRU-ordered, holds sheets that have been requested again since admission.
+
+A newly admitted sheet enters the probationary queue. When a request is served a cached sheet from the probationary queue, the sheet is promoted to the protected queue. When a request is served a cached sheet already in the protected queue, the sheet becomes the most recently used. Eviction takes the head of the probationary queue first; only when the probationary queue is empty does it take the least-recently-used entry of the protected queue.
+
+The protected queue is capped at a share of the 12 GiB budget, with the remainder available to the probationary queue. The share is a tunable parameter. Only the worker's private bookkeeping is affected: the HTTP contract, render-queue concurrency, and disk bound are unchanged.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q — probationary FIFO queue plus protected LRU queue | Plain LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q keeps repeatedly compared sheets warm while a long screening streams one-use sheets through the probationary queue; the cost is a second queue and a size-split parameter. LRU follows recent work cheaply but a screening evicts the comparison set; aged frequency counters need maintenance and can lag a project switch. All three touch only the worker's private bookkeeping, so none changes the fixed HTTP contract. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Eviction policy: 2Q (probationary FIFO plus protected LRU), chosen over plain LRU and aged frequency counting.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. The protected/probationary size split — the share of the 12 GiB budget reserved for the protected queue — is set during implementation from observed screening and comparison workloads.
